.\" tmux-companion(1) -- the manual.
.\"
.\" mdoc, the macro set tmux's own page uses, so the two read alike when
.\" somebody has both open. Render it without installing:
.\"
.\"     man ./docs/tmux-companion.1
.\"
.\" Every subcommand here is checked against the binary by a test, so a
.\" command added without a paragraph in this file fails the build.
.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt TMUX-COMPANION 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tmux-companion
.Nd a status line, a set of pickers and a project manager for tmux
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar flags
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is one binary in two modes.
.Pp
.Ic server
binds a unix socket and answers requests forever, holding the caches that
make a status line cheap.
Every other command is a client: it connects, starting the server when the
socket is absent, writes one JSON line, reads one back, prints it and exits.
.Pp
The point of the split is the status bar.
tmux gates
.Ql #()
to
.Ev status-interval
per attached client, and a spawn costs 12.4 ms of CPU before the command has
computed anything, so the bill is the number of distinct commands rather than
what they do.
.Ic status-right
answers with git, bandwidth and battery in one call.
.Pp
Commands that draw on a terminal -- the pickers, the dialogs -- run in the
client process.
The daemon has no terminal; it answers with rows and the client draws them.
.Ss Daemon lifetime
One daemon answers for one socket.
A start takes an exclusive lock on
.Pa <socket>.lock
before it touches the socket file, and a start that cannot take the lock exits
quietly, leaving the daemon that holds it to answer.
Without the lock two starts in the same moment both unlink the socket file and
both bind, and the one whose file was taken away keeps running where no client
can reach it.
.Pp
A daemon exits when the socket file it bound is deleted or replaced, which it
checks every thirty seconds.
That is the only thing that ends a daemon besides a signal or
.Ic __shutdown ,
and it is what stops an abandoned daemon from outliving the socket it was
started for.
The next client starts a fresh one, which costs one cold
.Xr git 1
status.
.Pp
.Ic __shutdown
removes the socket file on its way out, so a path with no socket file means no
daemon rather than a daemon that has stopped answering.
.Sh GETTING IN
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic start Oo Ar directory Oc Op Fl -last
The way in, from a shell that is not in tmux yet.
Opens the project picker, live sessions first and every directory the
configured source knows underneath, and attaches to what is chosen.
The source is
.Xr zoxide 1
unless
.Ic [project] dirs_source
says otherwise.
.Fl -last
goes back to the session used most recently without asking, and a directory
skips the picker.
Inside tmux it switches rather than attaching.
.Pp
.Ql tmux
on its own leaves you in a session called
.Ql 0
holding one bare shell, which is what this replaces.
.El
.Sh STATUS LINE COMMANDS
These print tmux format strings on standard output and are meant for
.Ql #()
in
.Pa tmux.conf .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic status-right Oo Ar path Oc Op Fl -branch-max-len Ar n
Git, bandwidth and battery in one call, computed concurrently.
One
.Ql #()
rather than three.
Takes a path and deliberately no pane pid: a pid makes the git segment
enumerate every process on the machine, which cost 18.5 ms of its 26.0.
.It Ic gst Oo Ar path Oc Oo Ar pid Oc Op Fl -no-daemon Fl -no-tmux
The git segment on its own: branch, what is dirty, ahead and behind.
With a pid it also marks a suspended editor.
.It Ic battery
Charge, as an icon and a percentage.
Reads the platform's battery and prints nothing on a machine without one.
.It Ic net Op Fl -no-daemon Fl -no-tmux
Bytes per second in and out, over all interfaces but the loopback.
A rate, so it is never cached.
Draws nothing below
.Ic [network] threshold_bps .
.It Ic sh-jobs Ar pane-pid
What is stopped or running under a pane, from the table in
.Ic [[sh_jobs.job]] .
.It Ic clients Ar attached Ar active
A marker when more than one client is looking at this session.
.It Ic window Fl i Ar index Op Ar flags
One window's entry for
.Ev window-status-format .
Costs a process spawn per window per redraw, and tmux redraws on pane output
as well as on the timer, so this is the expensive way to draw a window list.
.It Ic preview
Sample git segments in every colour style, without a server.
.El
.Ss Outside tmux
.Ic gst
and
.Ic net
are ordinary commands that print a string, and neither needs tmux to be
running.
Two flags make them usable anywhere a shell prompt, a bar or a script wants
them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -no-tmux
Write ANSI escapes instead of tmux's
.Ql #[fg=...]
markup, and reset the terminal at the end so nothing after the segment
inherits its colour.
A segment that drew nothing prints nothing, not even a newline.
.It Fl -no-daemon
Compute the answer in this process and exit.
No socket is opened, no server is started and nothing is left running.
The cost is the cache: every call pays for a cold
.Xr git 1
status, about 51 ms against a large tree, where the daemon answers a warm one
in well under a millisecond.
That is the right trade for a shell prompt and the wrong one for a status bar
refreshing once a second.
.Pp
.Ic net
is a rate and needs two counter readings, so without a daemon to hold the
first one it is kept in
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/net-sample .
The first call after a reboot records the reading and draws nothing, which is
what the daemon does on its first call and for the same reason.
.El
.Pp
For a prompt, in
.Xr bash 1
or
.Xr zsh 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PS1='$(tmux-companion gst --no-daemon --no-tmux) $ '
.Ed
.Pp
Inside tmux, leave both flags off: the daemon is what makes the segment cost
almost nothing to draw once a second.
.Sh PICKERS
Each is a fuzzy-matched list with a preview.
They are bound to keys in
.Pa docs/tmux.conf.full.example ;
the bindings shown are that file's.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic project Oo Ar directory Oc Op Fl -print
Live sessions and the directories the configured source knows, in one list,
each in its project's colour.
The source is
.Xr zoxide 1
by default and
.Ic [project] dirs_source
changes it; a source that is not installed lists nothing rather than failing,
and a directory it has never seen can still be typed in full.
Picking a live session switches to it; picking a directory builds its session
from
.Ic [[layout]] .
Bound to
.Ql M-s .
.It Ic project save Op Fl -no-commands
Capture this session's windows and panes as the layout this project opens
with.
A saved layout wins over the config.
The save is all or nothing: if tmux cannot be read, or answers with a line this
cannot parse, nothing is written and the layout already on disk is left alone,
because a layout quietly missing a pane is worse than one that is out of date.
The file is written through a temporary name and renamed over the old one, so a
save interrupted halfway leaves the previous layout rather than half of a new
one.
Bound to
.Ql prefix S .
.It Ic project forget
Delete the saved layout, so the config decides again.
Bound to
.Ql prefix M-S .
.It Ic project show
Which layout this project gets, which file decided, and the windows it opens.
.Pp
.Ic close-project
is the old name for
.Ic project close
and still works for one release.
.It Ic sessions save Oo Fl -skip-pane-history Oc Op Fl -exclude Ar names
Capture every session on the server as a new generation under the state
directory.
Where
.Ic project save
keeps one file per project, overwritten on demand and edited by hand, this
keeps generations of the whole server and throws the oldest away: the first
answers what a project looks like, the second what you were doing at the time.
The save is all or nothing, like a project's: a line of tmux output this cannot
read stops the write rather than shrinking the snapshot.
.Fl -skip-pane-history
records the sessions and leaves out what was on each pane's screen, which is the
expensive half and the half holding whatever you printed.
.Fl -exclude
adds to
.Ic [sessions] exclude
rather than replacing it, and a session left out is one that does not come back.
.It Ic sessions resurrect Oo Ar stamp Oc Oo Fl -only Ar names Oc Oo Fl -exclude Ar names Oc Oo Fl -merge Oc Oo Fl -dry-run Oc Oo Fl -yes Oc Op Fl -detach
Rebuild a server from a generation, defaulting to the newest.
With no generation of its own to read, it reads tmux-resurrect's newest save
instead, so a machine with months of those loses none of them on the day it
switches over.
Refuses outright when the server already holds sessions, naming them, because a
restore that half-landed on work somebody was doing is the failure worth being
careful about.
.Fl -merge
adds the sessions that are missing and never touches one that is running.
.Pp
What each pane gets told to run comes from
.Ic [[restore.program]] ,
which is default deny: a command no row claims opens the pane and says so
rather than running it.
So does a command the capture had to guess at, since its arguments are already
gone and running it would bring an agent back in the wrong conversation.
.Fl -dry-run
prints the exact tmux commands and exits, and they are the commands that would
run rather than a description of them.
It prints the waits too, as comments, because a restore is not only tmux calls.
.Pp
When the restore does not know what a pane should run, and somebody is watching,
it shows what it is about to do and counts down before going ahead.
It opens for a pane no row claims, one a row refused, and one whose command the
capture read off a running process so the arguments are already gone.
It never opens on a count of panes: a restore where everything is known goes
straight through however many there are, because a confirmation on every restore
is one people turn off.
Any key stops the clock,
.Ql tab
runs one of the listed panes after all,
.Ql a
takes all of them and
.Ql q
cancels.
.Ic [sessions] confirm_secs
sets the countdown and zero never draws it.
.Fl -yes
runs everything the table claimed without asking; whatever is not run still
opens its pane, in the right directory, at a prompt.
.Pp
Before a pane is told to run anything it has to have drawn a prompt, which tmux
knows only when the shell tells it, which is what
.Ic shell-init
makes the shell do.
A shell that emits the mark answers in milliseconds; one with no
.Ic shell-init
line in its rc file costs two seconds and is then typed into anyway, which is
what a restore did before the marks existed.
.Pp
It attaches at the end when somebody is watching, which means stdin is a
terminal and
.Ev TMUX
is unset, so the same command in a boot script leaves the server running.
.Fl -detach
says so explicitly.
The exit codes are
.Ql 0
restored,
.Ql 2
bad arguments,
.Ql 3
refused because sessions are live,
.Ql 4
nothing to restore, and
.Ql 1
for anything else.
.It Ic sessions shutdown Oo Fl -exclude Ar names Oc Oo Fl -daemon-too Oc Op Fl -dry-run
Save every session, then stop the tmux server.
.Fl -exclude
is not "leave these alone": stopping the server takes every session with it
either way, so a session left out here is one that does not come back, and the
command says which before it acts.
.Fl -daemon-too
stops the tmux-companion daemon as well.
.It Ic sessions restart Oo Fl -exclude Ar names Oc Oo Fl -keep-daemon Oc Op Fl -dry-run
The same, and then bring the server back with what it had.
It stops the daemon by default, which
.Fl -keep-daemon
turns off.
The default is that way round because
.Pa config.toml
is read once when the daemon starts and held for its whole life, so a restart
that left it running would hand back a new binary, a freshly sourced
.Pa tmux.conf ,
and yesterday's configuration.
.Pp
Both refuse to run from inside tmux, because stopping the server would take the
pane they were typed into and nothing after that would run.
There is no flag for it.
.It Ic sessions autosave Oo Fl -once Oc Op Fl -status
The snapshot timer the daemon runs, which
.Ic [sessions] autosave
turns on.
.Fl -once
takes one now and
.Fl -status
says when the last one happened, what the timer is set to, and whether the
daemon before this one stopped cleanly.
.Pp
That last answer comes from a file the daemon writes when it starts and removes
when it is asked to stop, so one left behind means the daemon before this never
got to leave.
A snapshot's own record of a clean shutdown cannot answer it: a capture taken on
the timer says
.Ql no clean shutdown recorded
because the daemon does not know yet, and nothing goes back to correct the one
that turned out to be the last before a crash.
.It Ic sessions list Op Fl -json
Every generation, newest first, with how much each holds and whether the daemon
that wrote it went on to shut down cleanly.
The newest carries a
.Ql *  .
A generation is stamped in UTC, so on a machine well away from it the stamp and
the file's own modification time are hours apart and both are right.
.It Ic sessions show Oo Ar stamp Oc Op Fl -json
What one generation holds, down to each pane's directory and command, defaulting
to the newest.
The command is marked with how sure the capture was of it:
.Ql Exact
for what the pane was started with,
.Ql Guessed
for what it happened to be running, and a shell for a pane sitting at a prompt.
.It Ic shutdown
Stop the tmux-companion daemon.
tmux is not touched, and the next client starts a new one.
.It Ic restart
Stop the daemon and start a fresh one, which is how a change to
.Pa config.toml
takes effect: the file is read once at startup and held for the daemon's whole
life.
tmux is not touched.
.It Ic keys Oo Ar query Oc Op Fl -all , Fl -print
Every binding tmux knows, searchable, with what it runs in the preview.
Enter runs the binding.
Bound to
.Ql prefix ? .
.It Ic cheatsheet Op Fl -plain
The bindings written in
.Pa tmux.conf ,
in four boxes, most-used first.
A box with fewer than three of its own is filled out from tmux's own noted
bindings.
Bound to
.Ql prefix C-c .
.It Ic new-window
A window here, or at any directory.
The rows are the pane's own directory and then the same source
.Ic project
lists, so it is the one setting for both.
The query starts on the pane's own directory, which is also the first row, so
the key and enter is another window here.
A path the source has never seen opens when it is typed in full.
Bound to
.Ql prefix c .
.It Ic run
A command from shell history, run in a pane that slides out beside this one
and waits for an answer before closing.
Bound to
.Ql prefix e .
.It Ic theme pick Op Fl -themes Ar dir
Every theme with its colour beside it, applied on the spot and remembered
against the project.
Bound to
.Ql prefix C-t .
.El
.Sh SESSIONS AND WINDOWS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic toggle Ar session Ar window
Move to the next window in this session's layout, and back again.
Bound to
.Ql M-a .
.It Ic project close Oo Ar session Oc Op Fl -discard , Fl -no-save
Close a project by asking every window to exit, capturing the layout on the
way out.
.Fl -discard
quits editors with
.Ql :qa!
and throws away unsaved work.
Bound to
.Ql prefix X .
.It Ic zen Op Fl -pane Ar id
Clear everything but the pane you are working in.
With other panes open that is tmux's own zoom: this pane fills the window, the
rest are hidden, and the same key puts the layout back untouched.
With no other panes there is nothing to zoom -- a lone pane already fills the
window, and tmux's own
.Ql prefix z
does nothing at all there -- so the key takes the status bar instead, which is
the only clutter left.
One key for "give me the most screen you can", whatever the window looks like.
.Ic zoom
is the old name and works for one more release.
Bound to
.Ql prefix z .
.It Ic autosave Op Fl -once , Fl -status
The session-list autosave the daemon runs.
.El
.Sh COPY MODE AND OPENING
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic open Oo Ar text Oc Op Fl s , Fl i , Fl n , Fl d Ar dir , Fl -pane Ar id , Fl -cursor-x Ar column
Open what is in the text: a URL goes to a browser, a
.Ql path:line:column
opens an editor there.
.Fl -cursor-x
names the column the cursor is in, and what sits under it wins.
The shipped binding passes the line under the cursor together with
.Ql #{copy_cursor_x} ,
so nothing has to be selected first: being on the thing is the whole gesture,
and a line naming two paths opens the one the cursor is actually on rather than
whichever came first.
.Fl s
reads the tmux selection instead of arguments, for a binding that would rather
select a range by hand.
.Fl i ,
or
.Fl -choose ,
asks which application opens it, from the
.Ic [[open.application]]
list.
With nothing in that list there is nothing to choose between, so it opens what
it would have opened anyway rather than showing an empty picker.
.Fl n
prints what it would open and opens nothing.
.Fl -pane
names the pane this is for, and the shipped binding passes
.Ql #{pane_id}
so that it does.
tmux runs the binding through
.Ic run-shell ,
where
.Ev TMUX_PANE
holds the most recently active pane on the server rather than the pane the key
was pressed in, so without it the editor can open in a window nobody is looking
at.
Bound to
.Ql o
in copy mode.
.It Ic clipboard
Copy standard input to the system clipboard, whichever command this platform
has.
Bound to
.Ql y
in copy mode.
.It Ic shell-init Ar shell
Print the shell code that emits the OSC 133 prompt marks.
tmux has had
.Ic next-prompt
and
.Ic previous-prompt
since 3.3 and they do nothing until the shell says where a prompt begins.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
eval "$(tmux-companion shell-init zsh)"     # or bash
tmux-companion shell-init fish | source
.Ed
.El
.Sh THEMES
A theme is a file of tmux options that
.Pa _apply.tmux
turns into colours.
Options are set without
.Fl g ,
so a theme belongs to one session and two projects can be different colours
at once.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic theme init Op Fl -themes Ar dir
Write the starter themes and the two files that apply them.
Six colours, which
.Ic theme gen Fl -shades
grows to 151.
Nothing is overwritten.
.It Ic theme gen Oo Fl -apply Oc Oo Fl -shades Oc Op Fl -background Ar colour
Compute each theme's readable text colour and a visible border.
Without
.Fl -apply
it reports what would change and touches nothing.
.Fl -shades Op Ar level
writes a theme for every colour in the 6x6x6 cube whose text clears a contrast
floor, named after the bundled colour each sits nearest to, so
.Ql ember-04
and
.Ql pine-11
land together in the picker.
.Ar level
is a rung of a ladder, and the flag on its own means
.Ql aaa :
.Bl -column "aaa " "12:1 " "themes" -compact -offset indent
.It Ic aa Ta 4.5:1 Ta 216
.It Ic aaa Ta 7:1 Ta 151
.It Ic a4 Ta 9.5:1 Ta 105
.It Ic a5 Ta 12:1 Ta 75
.It Ic a6 Ta \(em Ta 18
.El
.Pp
WCAG names the first two rungs and stops; the rest continue at its own spacing
of 2.5 per step, so the ladder is one rule rather than four opinions.
.Ic a6
is not a floor at all: it is the bundled six with a lighter and a darker
sibling of each, which is what
.Fl -shades
did before it swept the cube, and the only rung whose colours a person chose.
.Ic aaa
is the default rather than
.Ic aa
because the worst colour in the cube scores 4.60:1, so an AA filter keeps all
216 of them
.Pq a threshold that is arithmetically a no-op .
The text colour is chosen to clear 4.5:1 against the theme's own background
and the border to clear 3:1 against the terminal's, so a theme that cannot be
read cannot be written.
.It Ic theme add Fl -bg Ar colour Op Fl -fg Ar colour
A theme from one colour.
The text colour is computed unless given, and a given pair under 4.5:1 is
refused without
.Fl -force .
.It Ic theme apply Oo Ar session Oc Op Fl t Ar target , Fl -all
The theme a session should have, without asking.
Run from the
.Ic session-created
hook.
Silent when there is no theme to apply, which is every install until
.Ic theme init
has run.
.Fl -all
repaints every session instead of one, which is what a reload needs: sourcing
.Pa tmux.conf
resets the global options a theme sets, so without it every session is left
wearing whatever the file says rather than its own colour.
.It Ic theme list-colours
Every colour tmux takes, painted, with its hex and the contrast on it.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
TOML, read from the first of:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
.Ev TMUX_COMPANION_CONFIG
.It
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux-companion/config.toml
.It
.Pa ~/.config/tmux-companion/config.toml
.It
.Pa ~/.tmux-companion.toml
.El
.Pp
Every setting with its default, annotated, is
.Pa docs/config.example.toml .
The sections are
.Ic [general] , [dirs] , [git] , [network] , [battery] , [glyphs] ,
.Ic [status] , [sh_jobs] , [usage] , [[layout]] , [autoreload] ,
.Ic [window_names] , [notify] , [project] , [autosave] , [sessions] ,
.Ic [[restore.program]] , [run] , [clipboard] , [theme] , [bar] , [picker]
and
.Ic [open] .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic config path
Which file is being read.
.It Ic config check
Parse it and say what is wrong, with the line and a suggestion for a
misspelled key or an unrecognised value.
It also names any setting that still works but is deprecated.
.It Ic config dump
Every setting with its default, as a config file.
.El
.Pp
Two settings are worth knowing before the rest.
.Ic [bar] background
is what the segments draw their caps against and has to match
.Ev status-style ;
it used to be a compiled-in
.Ql colour233 ,
which is one person's bar and nobody else's.
.Ic [picker] preview
is where every picker puts its preview pane, or
.Ql none
for no preview at all.
The rest of that section is what a picker looks like rather than what it holds:
.Ic border ,
.Ic label_position ,
.Ic hint_position ,
.Ic prompt_position ,
.Ic list_from ,
.Ic counter ,
.Ic rules ,
.Ic marker ,
.Ic column_order
and the preview's own label.
Each of them can be written again under
.Ic [picker.keys] , [picker.project] , [picker.window] , [picker.theme] ,
.Ic [picker.run]
or
.Ic [picker.open]
for the one picker that wants a different answer, together with the
.Ic label , hint
and
.Ic preview_label
that picker says.
How big the popup itself is belongs to
.Ic display-popup
in
.Pa tmux.conf ,
not here.
.Ic [open] split
is which side the editor opens on,
.Ql right
or
.Ql bottom .
.Pp
.Ic [project] dirs_source
is where the project picker gets its directories, one of
.Ql zoxide ,
.Ql z ,
.Ql cdr ,
.Ql ghq
or
.Ql none .
.Xr zoxide 1
is the default rather than a requirement, and nothing here has to be installed:
a source that is absent lists nothing, leaving live sessions and whatever is
typed.
.Ql z
reads the
.Pa ~/.z
database and
.Ql cdr
reads
.Pa ~/.chpwd-recent-dirs ,
as files rather than as commands, because both are shell functions that cannot
be run from here.
.Ic [project] dirs_command
takes any command printing one absolute path per line, as a list of words, and
wins over
.Ic dirs_source ;
.Ic [project] visit_command
is what records a pick, defaulting to
.Ql zoxide add
for zoxide and nothing for the rest.
.Ic [project] zoxide
is the old spelling of
.Ql dirs_source = \(dqnone\(dq
and goes away in the next release.
.Pp
.Ic [[git.branch_types]]
is the glyph drawn before a branch name, keyed on how the name starts.
Each entry is an
.Ic icon
and a list of
.Ic prefixes ,
tried in the order written; the first prefix that claims the name wins, and
it is cut off the name the bar draws, so
.Ql feat/status-bar
renders as the feature glyph and
.Ql status-bar .
Prefixes are compared without regard to case and are plain text rather than
regular expressions.
.Ic icon
takes
.Ql {NAME}
for any glyph the tool knows, so the file stays readable without a patched
font, and draws anything else as written.
The list replaces the six shipped entries rather than adding to them;
.Ic config dump
prints them in the shape they are edited in.
A name no entry claims keeps the plain branch glyph, which is why
.Ql main
and
.Ql master
need no entry.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic doctor
What a bug report needs: the version, the socket, what the config says, what
tmux says, and what is missing.
.It Ic probe
What the terminal answers about itself.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "$XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/projects/" -compact
.It Pa ~/.config/tmux-companion/config.toml
the configuration
.It Pa ~/.config/tmux/themes/
themes, and the two files that apply them
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/projects/
saved per-project layouts
.It Pa /tmp/tmux-companion-$UID.sock
the daemon's socket
.It Pa /tmp/tmux-companion-$UID.sock.lock
the lock one daemon holds for the life of the process, so a second start
cannot unlink the socket out from under the first
.It Pa ~/.z
the z database, read when
.Ic [project] dirs_source
is
.Ql z
.It Pa ~/.chpwd-recent-dirs
zsh's recent directories, read when
.Ic [project] dirs_source
is
.Ql cdr
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TMUX_COMPANION_CONFIG -compact
.It Ev TMUX_COMPANION_CONFIG
a configuration file, before the search order
.It Ev TMUX_COMPANION_SOCK
the daemon socket, before the default
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME , XDG_STATE_HOME
where configuration and state live
.It Ev _Z_DATA
